1
00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:03.670
In this lesson, you'll learn how to parse documents

2
00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:05.972
using optical character recognition

3
00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:09.476
and how to integrate its results into agentic workflows.

4
00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:14.047
You'll build an agent to parse text from documents using OCR,

5
00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:17.851
and then extract information from the text using LLMs.

6
00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:22.055
Along the way, you'll identify cases like handwriting, tables,

7
00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:25.859
and scanned images that make these steps really challenging.

8
00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:31.197
Let's dive in. Today is lesson one, document processing basics.

9
00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:34.801
We're going to start simple today, but everything we cover is foundational

10
00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:39.205
to the production grade systems used by enterprises and developers today,

11
00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:41.908
which we will cover in subsequent lessons.

12
00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.477
So here's the agenda for today.

13
00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:47.080
Processing what it is and why it matters.

14
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.582
Parsing, extracting and output formats,

15
00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:52.285
JSON, Markdown, and how they are used.

16
00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.888
OCR, how it works under the hood

17
00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:58.291
and its workflow and limitations where it can break.

18
00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:00.994
Agentic AI and the ReAct framework.

19
00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:03.863
adding a brain on top of OCR,

20
00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:06.966
and we'll review some practical demos and failure modes

21
00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.669
and what still challenges real systems.

22
00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.538
And after all that, we'll roll

23
00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:13.173
up our sleeves and do some coding

24
00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:16.176
where you'll build your first simple document agent.

25
00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:20.080
You'll notice this is a bottom-up journey from pixels, text,

26
00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:22.348
to structure and then reasoning.

27
00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:24.551
Let's ground this in the real world

28
00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:26.252
pain these systems are meant to solve.

29
00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:29.856
Modern organizations are flooded with digital documents,

30
00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:32.959
invoices, receipts, contracts and reports.

31
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.564
These documents live in a massive digital filing cabinet,

32
00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:41.968
usually as PDFs, PowerPoints, Word docs, or even images.

33
00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.504
They're built for human eyes, not machines,

34
00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:48.508
which means the information is hard to search, hard to analyze,

35
00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:51.111
and of course, hard to automate.

36
00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:54.314
If your data is trapped inside unstructured documents,

37
00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:57.817
someone has to manually open, read, and retype it

38
00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:01.121
into a different system, which does not scale.

39
00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.591
So if we want to use this information in analytics,

40
00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:07.293
automation, or AI, we need to convert

41
00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:09.963
unstructured documents into structured,

42
00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.965
machine-readable data.

43
00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.267
So, what does a solution look like?

44
00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.970
Document processing is turning unstructured documents

45
00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.772
into structured, machine-readable data,

46
00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.975
typically JSON or Markdown.

47
00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.277
It's more than just grabbing text.

48
00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:26.479
Parsing must understand what pieces of text

49
00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:28.681
actually mean, how they're related,

50
00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:32.285
and how to organize them into predictable structure.

51
00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:33.953
If you're parsing an invoice, you

52
00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:35.655
don't just want a blob of text.

53
00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.324
You want to extract the vendor name,

54
00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.660
invoice date, total amount, and line items.

55
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.663
Extracting assumes text is already machine-readable.

56
00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:46.266
But if your document is a scan or a photo,

57
00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:48.668
the computer sees only image pixels.

58
00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.171
So before extracting can happen, we

59
00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:53.873
need OCR to turn pixels into text.

60
00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:55.942
How do we represent the output?

61
00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:59.145
Parsing and extracting typically produce two useful formats.

62
00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:01.447
First is Markdown or HTML,

63
00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:05.151
which is designed for humans like us and LLMs.

64
00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:09.355
This preserves structures like headers, tables, and lists,

65
00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:13.660
and is perfect for feeding into LLMs or showing to end users.

66
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.862
Second is JSON for machine

67
00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:17.964
and APIs. It is hierarchical,

68
00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:19.933
easy to traverse programmatically,

69
00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:22.635
and perfect for downstream pipelines and applications.

70
00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:25.638
So remember, JSON is for machines,

71
00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:29.642
Markdown or HTML is for humans and LLMs.

72
00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:33.646
Good rule of thumb, if you're thinking about analytics or databases,

73
00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:35.848
JSON is a great option.

74
00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.651
and if you're thinking about building a RAG solution

75
00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.353
or a chat user interface,

76
00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:43.756
Markdown or HTML could be a great option.

77
00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.426
But all of this assumes we already have the text.

78
00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:48.828
What if all we have is just pixels?

79
00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:52.332
OCR or optical character recognition

80
00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:55.535
is the technology that takes an image of text

81
00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:58.738
and converts it into machine-readable text.

82
00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:01.107
It typically works in two steps.

83
00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.176
First is image cleanup,

84
00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:06.980
so deskewing, denoising, or contrast adjustment.

85
00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.983
Then, text recognition. Pattern matching.

86
00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:12.885
Does this shape look like an eight or a B?

87
00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:17.090
Once it recognizes the text, it will produce editable text

88
00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.893
as a digital output or a searchable PDF.

89
00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:25.198
But we also need to be honest about what OCR cannot do.

90
00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:29.802
OCR is really great at reading clean documents,

91
00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:34.207
but it does not understand structure, meaning, or relationships.

92
00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:37.944
After OCR, you typically get a wall of text.

93
00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:41.247
If you want to find totals, extract tables,

94
00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:44.851
identify headings, and classify documents

95
00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:47.453
you need intelligence on top of OCR.

96
00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:51.758
You can think of OCR as the eyes, but not the brain.

97
00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:56.162
And beyond its lack of understanding, it could also just fail.

98
00:04:56.763 --> 00:04:58.765
OCR breaks in predictable ways.

99
00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:03.236
Poor image quality, blurry photos, shadows, and noise.

100
00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:05.638
Complex layouts and skews,

101
00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.341
so multi-column text, nested tables,

102
00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:13.313
And of course non-standard text like handwriting, stamps, stylized fonts.

103
00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:18.017
These failures cascade down into parsing and extraction errors.

104
00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:20.119
So OCR is necessary

105
00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:23.923
but nowhere near sufficient for full document understanding.

106
00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:27.927
So if you've ever tried to OCR a picture of a receipt

107
00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:30.463
taken in a dim restaurant, you've probably

108
00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:32.899
experienced all three failure modes at once.

109
00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.768
All of this leads to a key idea.

110
00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:38.671
Processing is not the same as understanding.

111
00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:41.974
OCR can read characters, but it doesn't comprehend them.

112
00:05:42.275 --> 00:05:45.578
It doesn't know what is a header versus a value.

113
00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:48.214
which number is the total

114
00:05:48.715 --> 00:05:51.617
or whether text belongs in a table or a footnote.

115
00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:55.922
OCR gives you perception, pixels to character,

116
00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:57.623
but no cognitive layer.

117
00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:02.128
To turn that wall of text into meaningful, structured data,

118
00:06:02.128 --> 00:06:05.932
we need a brain. And that's where agentic AI comes in.

119
00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:09.902
Agentic AI adds the missing cognitive layer.

120
00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:12.438
An agent is an autonomous system

121
00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:14.474
that can perceive its environment,

122
00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:17.643
reason about goals, and take certain actions.

123
00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:20.380
In document processing specifically,

124
00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.783
the agent reads the document via OCR if needed,

125
00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:26.185
thinks about what the user asked,

126
00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:28.187
chooses which tools to call,

127
00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:30.690
and iterates until it reaches the goal.

128
00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.193
If the OCR is the eyes, the agent is the brain.

129
00:06:34.694 --> 00:06:36.996
And rule-based pipelines can collapse

130
00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:38.698
the moment you hit an edge case,

131
00:06:39.098 --> 00:06:41.401
whereas agents can reason through them.

132
00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:44.604
So what does an agentic system look like under the hood?

133
00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:47.607
Agentic document systems typically have three parts.

134
00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:50.610
The brain, using an LLM,

135
00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:54.614
which is responsible for reasoning, planning, and decision making.

136
00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:57.016
the eyes or OCR,

137
00:06:57.417 --> 00:07:00.420
which converts visual content into text.

138
00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:04.123
And the hands, or the tools that agents can use.

139
00:07:04.524 --> 00:07:06.726
APIs, database lookups,

140
00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:09.929
file operations and function calls.

141
00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:14.066
And when all these three things are wired together,

142
00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:17.036
you can say find the total amount on this invoice.

143
00:07:17.737 --> 00:07:21.040
Then the agent will decide to run OCR,

144
00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.044
inspect the text, locate the total and return the answer.

145
00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:28.915
You don't have to hardcode every step or every edge case.

146
00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:33.119
This mental model, brain, eyes, and hands,

147
00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:38.119
will appear again and again in subsequent labs and lessons.

148
00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:43.162
But how does the agent think, not just once, but step-by-step.

149
00:07:43.463 --> 00:07:45.765
The ReAct, reason and act framework

150
00:07:45.865 --> 00:07:48.067
describes how agents think.

151
00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:51.170
First is thought. What do I need to do next?

152
00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:53.940
Then it will take a certain action,

153
00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.643
choose and call a set of tools that it needs.

154
00:07:58.044 --> 00:08:00.246
And once this is done, it will go through

155
00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:03.049
an observation process to examine the result

156
00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:08.020
and repeat. Think, act, observe, and think again.

157
00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:12.024
This loop gives agents agency, adaptability,

158
00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:14.527
and the ability to correct mistakes.

159
00:08:15.328 --> 00:08:20.328
Most modern agentic frameworks are built on some variation of ReAct.

160
00:08:20.867 --> 00:08:24.470
And of course, one major benefit is that it's debuggable.

161
00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.740
You can literally read the agent's thoughts and tool calls.

162
00:08:28.441 --> 00:08:31.244
All right, that's enough theory, let's build something.

163
00:08:31.544 --> 00:08:33.646
Now it's lab time.

164
00:08:33.646 --> 00:08:35.848
We're going to take everything we covered on the slides,

165
00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:38.551
parsing, OCR, and agentic reasoning,

166
00:08:38.551 --> 00:08:41.320
and build a simple document agent together.

167
00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:44.156
By the end of the lab, you'll have an agent

168
00:08:44.156 --> 00:08:45.825
that can read a document,

169
00:08:45.825 --> 00:08:47.627
use OCR as a tool,

170
00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:49.829
extract structured information.

171
00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:51.831
And don't worry if this is your first time,

172
00:08:51.831 --> 00:08:55.034
we'll go step by step. Let's switch to the notebook.
